{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 22",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
